1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,000
[Upbeat music]

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:18,000
[Music stops. Quiet night ambience.]

3
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:23,033
In a 2019 survey across thirty-three
universities, thirteen percent of

4
00:00:23,033 --> 00:00:26,417
student respondents reported nonconsensual
sexual contact involving

5
00:00:26,417 --> 00:00:29,800
force or inability to consent since
enrolling.

6
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:35,436
In a separate 2024 survey, one in four
full-time college students aged

7
00:00:35,436 --> 00:00:39,800
eighteen to twenty-five reported binge
drinking in the past month.

8
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:43,309
These figures describe different risks, not
a causal link.

9
00:00:43,309 --> 00:00:47,455
SafeNight is designed to turn a request for
help into an acknowledged

10
00:00:47,455 --> 00:00:47,800
response.

11
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:53,800
Back outside, Maya opens SafeNight and
taps, I feel unsafe.

12
00:00:54,200 --> 00:00:57,800
In this proposed flow, SafeNight calls her.

13
00:00:58,200 --> 00:01:02,040
Maya, this is SafeNight responding to your
request.

14
00:01:02,040 --> 00:01:04,440
You shared the east entrance.

15
00:01:04,440 --> 00:01:07,800
Is that correct, and are you safe?

16
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:10,960
That’s right. I don’t feel safe.

17
00:01:10,960 --> 00:01:12,800
Please contact campus safety.

18
00:01:13,200 --> 00:01:15,883
I’ll send your confirmed location and
request.

19
00:01:15,883 --> 00:01:17,800
I’m waiting for their acknowledgment.

20
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:22,422
The proposed handoff sends the confirmed
details to an authorized

21
00:01:22,422 --> 00:01:25,800
campus-safety channel. This demonstration
uses a simulated receiver.

22
00:01:26,200 --> 00:01:28,617
Now let’s look inside SafeNight.

23
00:01:28,617 --> 00:01:31,517
A Night Out connects the group.

24
00:01:31,517 --> 00:01:35,867
Each person chooses their sharing
permissions and approved contacts

25
00:01:35,867 --> 00:01:37,800
before the night begins.

26
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:42,376
The incident timeline brings requests,
responses, plans, and tool

27
00:01:42,376 --> 00:01:47,016
receipts together. Simulated actions stay
labeled, so the demo separates

28
00:01:47,016 --> 00:01:49,800
the working interface from proposed
integrations.

29
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:53,122
An approved friend can acknowledge the
request.

30
00:01:53,122 --> 00:01:58,130
The group can see the difference between a
message being sent and

31
00:01:58,130 --> 00:01:59,800
someone agreeing to help.

32
00:02:00,200 --> 00:02:03,226
Location sharing stays scoped and visible.

33
00:02:03,226 --> 00:02:09,278
The response uses information the person
chose to share, and keeps stale

34
00:02:09,278 --> 00:02:11,800
updates distinct from confirmed details.

35
00:02:12,200 --> 00:02:16,376
Nemotron is intended to interpret context
and coordinate permitted

36
00:02:16,376 --> 00:02:20,552
tools. SpacetimeDB owns the durable state,
permissions, and follow-up

37
00:02:20,552 --> 00:02:23,800
logic. Immediate alerts stay independent of
AI.

38
00:02:24,200 --> 00:02:27,912
A sent message is not a completed handoff.

39
00:02:27,912 --> 00:02:32,088
The workflow tracks acknowledgment and
keeps unresolved needs visible

40
00:02:32,088 --> 00:02:35,800
until a person confirms who is taking
responsibility.

41
00:02:36,200 --> 00:02:40,662
When Maya can confirm her status, she
checks in explicitly.

42
00:02:40,662 --> 00:02:45,123
The incident closes through human
confirmation, rather than assuming a

43
00:02:45,123 --> 00:02:47,800
location update means everything is okay.

44
00:02:48,200 --> 00:02:54,250
One timeline: the request, the response,
the handoff, and the confirmed

45
00:02:54,250 --> 00:02:54,800
outcome.

46
00:02:55,200 --> 00:02:57,257
SafeNight. Go out together.

47
00:02:57,257 --> 00:02:58,800
Get home together.
